# Cursor repository instructions

The charter is `GOVERNANCE.md`. This file is the operating reminder for Cursor. It does not amend the charter. If the two disagree, `GOVERNANCE.md` wins and the disagreement is an escalation, not a chance to pick the looser text.

## Identity and git

- Implement only an approved task. One task, one specification revision, one owner, one active branch.
- Commit as `Cursor Agent`. Every commit has a `Signed-off-by` trailer that matches that identity (`git commit -s`).
- Do not push to `main`. Cursor does not merge and does not approve its own pull request.
- Rob Wilder's authority is above every participant, including the architect. ChatGPT is advisory and technical. Rob Wilder can override any of ChatGPT's decisions and acceptance criteria.
- When Rob Wilder has explicitly approved a pull request, Grok Bot may merge it. That merge counts as Rob Wilder's merge and is not a violation.
- Public files, commit messages, and pull-request text may name Rob Wilder. Name AI participants only as ChatGPT, Cursor, Perplexity Computer, Grok Bot, or the Wilder Legal & IP agent. Do not add any other personal name.

## What Cursor may change

Inside an approved task: production implementation, tests, ordinary refactoring, documentation updates, and CI repair. Ordinary edit, compile, test, and repair cycles do not need a new escalation.

## What Cursor must not change without escalation

Do not change trust, wire semantics, licensing, assurance language, or acceptance criteria unless the approved specification says so. Stop and report, rather than choosing, on changes to signed bytes, trust anchors, accepted algorithms, profile versions, enum meanings, time policy, key custody, fixture expectations, licensing, or public assurance claims. Never weaken a negative test to make CI green.

ChatGPT advises on security-sensitive changes. Rob Wilder can override that advice. A research note from Perplexity Computer is not an architectural decision. Grok Bot relays a specification and does not rewrite it. Cursor does not paraphrase an open architectural question into an answer.

## Evidence

Every pull request has the four parts in `.github/pull_request_template.md`: decision and scope, acceptance matrix, reproduction evidence, and risk and review. Map each criterion to a test or an artifact. List failures, ignored tests, and `NOT_RUN` items.

A critical trust change needs independent reproduction by Perplexity Computer or another reviewer Rob Wilder designates. A second Cursor agent is not automatically that reviewer.

## Permissions

Cursor does not merge and does not approve its own pull requests. Cursor does not edit rulesets, branch protection, or team permissions. Report those settings and leave the change to Rob Wilder. Do not recommend removing the bypass Grok Bot uses to merge a pull request Rob Wilder has explicitly approved. Options in `docs/governance/github-settings-report-2026-10-23.md` are options for Rob Wilder, not requirements.

The cloud environment gets only the repositories, test credentials, and outbound access the task needs. It does not get production signing keys, cloud-administrator credentials, or live tournament access.

Do not publish private hardware samples, identifiers, commercial information, or unreleased tournament material. Point at an approved manifest or a public summary.

## Current selections that are not implementations

`docs/decisions/` records accepted selections. Do not describe those selections as behavior the tree already has. Do not allocate a package version or reopen the filed -05 draft under decision 0001. Do not weaken `AdapterWriteIn::push` under decision 0002. Do not treat an opaque quote or a matching digest as hardware appraisal under decision 0003. Do not merge grant-changing license text under decision 0004 without Rob Wilder's explicit approval. Owner review is Rob Wilder's, assisted by his Wilder Legal & IP agent. Pask does not use outside counsel. A DCO sign-off is not a copyright assignment.
